1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:05,580
Before we start the next section, I would love to share with you, when I became conscious

2
00:00:05,580 --> 00:00:14,099
of showmanship, there's one quote in the world of magic that revealed to me the skills I

3
00:00:14,099 --> 00:00:14,660
needed to learn.

4
00:00:16,019 --> 00:00:21,160
And the quote was this, the quote said that, it was by a magician named Robert Houdin,

5
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:30,899
Dan, who said, magic, a magician is just an actor playing the part of a magician.

6
00:00:31,179 --> 00:00:35,020
And I thought it was fantastic, so I thought that, oh, to become a better magician, I need

7
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:38,979
to go learn acting skills, I need to go to theatre, so I actually did a couple of years

8
00:00:38,979 --> 00:00:41,140
of theatre, did a couple of years of vocal classes too.

9
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:46,560
And my first vocal class was with my singing teacher, and I'll forever remember this experience.

10
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:50,740
I remember walking into the room, and she was sitting there with a piano, she was a

11
00:00:50,740 --> 00:00:51,140
bit elderly.

12
00:00:51,159 --> 00:00:58,320
And she didn't even look at me, so she just goes, come in, okay, so I come in, and I sit

13
00:00:58,320 --> 00:01:04,519
down, and then she goes, sit down, and she goes, tell me how you feel, young man, after

14
00:01:04,519 --> 00:01:04,859
this.

15
00:01:05,659 --> 00:01:12,000
And then she plays one key on the piano, non-stop, for at least three minutes.

16
00:01:12,879 --> 00:01:14,959
I was just like, what the hell is going on?

17
00:01:15,659 --> 00:01:18,840
And then she turns around and she goes, how do you feel, young man?

18
00:01:19,439 --> 00:01:20,980
I was going, actually.

19
00:01:21,159 --> 00:01:23,200
I was actually a little bit frightened, I feel like this is the ecstasy that's going

20
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:23,659
to happen now.

21
00:01:24,219 --> 00:01:25,500
And she says, good.

22
00:01:26,099 --> 00:01:26,900
She goes, good.

23
00:01:27,439 --> 00:01:29,760
And after she turns around again, she goes, listen to this.

24
00:01:30,260 --> 00:01:36,780
And then she plays this beautiful song, beautiful song, called Romance, about three minutes

25
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:37,159
in length.

26
00:01:37,599 --> 00:01:41,159
And then she turns around, she looks at me this time, and she says, how do you feel now?

27
00:01:41,959 --> 00:01:45,959
And I said, actually quite sad, it was actually a really traumatic song.

28
00:01:46,599 --> 00:01:50,359
And she goes, Vin, what you don't realise, what most people don't realise, is they go

29
00:01:50,359 --> 00:01:57,540
through their entire lives speaking like this, with just one key, not realising that they've

30
00:01:57,540 --> 00:01:59,640
got 88 keys on their piano.

31
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:02,879
I'm going to teach you how to play like this.

32
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:08,460
She was quite expensive, I gave her a lot of my money, but she changed my life.

33
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:15,439
And who better to start off our day officially than a vocal master, someone who understands

34
00:02:15,439 --> 00:02:16,159
the voice.

35
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:18,599
And that's who I want to introduce you to.

36
00:02:18,719 --> 00:02:20,199
This is Melissa Trinkman.

37
00:02:20,199 --> 00:02:22,599
Who's one of the best singing teachers in California.

38
00:02:23,060 --> 00:02:26,939
And we have the pleasure of having her today, so please can we give her a big round of applause

39
00:02:27,439 --> 00:02:28,379
as she comes on stage.

40
00:02:28,639 --> 00:02:29,379
Thank you.

41
00:02:30,319 --> 00:02:31,979
All right, I'm going to grab this.

42
00:02:34,340 --> 00:02:37,259
Hello, thank you for that great introduction, I love that story.

43
00:02:38,039 --> 00:02:39,539
My name is Melissa Trinkman.

44
00:02:40,039 --> 00:02:42,539
As Vin said, I'm a singing teacher.

45
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:44,319
That's one of my jobs.

46
00:02:44,979 --> 00:02:49,479
I work with people to help them reach their vocal potential, you could say.

47
00:02:50,199 --> 00:02:54,099
I've been training my own voice, though, for over 20 years.

48
00:02:54,979 --> 00:03:00,120
I'm an opera singer, so if any of you have ever seen an opera, I sing with the Los Angeles

49
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:00,539
Opera.

50
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:05,159
Opera's kind of like the Olympics of the voice, I would say.

51
00:03:05,699 --> 00:03:09,000
It's the type of singing that takes the most amount of training.

52
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:15,180
So when I'm not singing with the opera, I'm a voice coach.

53
00:03:15,699 --> 00:03:17,319
I also am a student.

54
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:20,180
I'm working on my doctorate in vocal training.

55
00:03:20,180 --> 00:03:22,620
I teach vocal performance at University of Southern California.

56
00:03:23,039 --> 00:03:25,819
Yes, you can get a doctorate in singing, who knew?

57
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:33,180
But one of the things that I do there is I study vocal pedagogy, which is the art and

58
00:03:33,180 --> 00:03:37,520
science of teaching about the voice, or teaching singing.

59
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:43,560
And so a lot of people, when they find out I'm a singing teacher, they say, can you really

60
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:45,000
teach anyone to sing?

61
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:48,000
Is it really possible that anyone can sing?

62
00:03:48,860 --> 00:03:50,159
I always say, yes.

63
00:03:50,159 --> 00:03:53,620
Well, if you can speak well, you can sing well.

64
00:03:55,219 --> 00:03:59,919
But that's kind of a big if, because a lot of people do not speak well.

65
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:02,780
And I include singers in that category.

66
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:10,319
And in fact, a lot of singers, when they have vocal problems, it's not because of the way

67
00:04:10,319 --> 00:04:10,879
that they're singing.

68
00:04:11,039 --> 00:04:13,580
It's because of issues in their speech.

69
00:04:14,840 --> 00:04:17,000
So yes, anyone can sing.

70
00:04:17,079 --> 00:04:19,600
But invariably, when I'm teaching a voice lesson.

71
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:20,680
I'm teaching someone to sing.

72
00:04:21,199 --> 00:04:23,680
I'm also working on their speaking voice at the same time.

73
00:04:24,040 --> 00:04:28,560
And then when I'm working with a speaker on their speaking voice, little do they know

74
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:30,519
it, but I'm also training them to be a singer.

75
00:04:31,920 --> 00:04:38,040
So it's kind of fun to get to see how the singing voice and the speaking voice interact.

76
00:04:39,120 --> 00:04:41,399
But today I wanted to start with a question for all of you.

77
00:04:41,899 --> 00:04:47,959
How many of you, when you hear your voice played back for you on a recording, don't

78
00:04:47,959 --> 00:04:48,860
like the sound of your voice?

79
00:04:50,439 --> 00:04:51,939
Oh my gosh, almost everyone.

80
00:04:52,579 --> 00:04:54,399
So that's pretty typical, actually.

81
00:04:54,540 --> 00:04:58,959
Studies have shown that people, when they hear their own voice, they first of all don't

82
00:04:58,959 --> 00:05:01,800
recognize it, and they don't like how it sounds.

83
00:05:02,519 --> 00:05:08,279
So my follow-up question, of course, is how many of you have actually been trained how

84
00:05:08,279 --> 00:05:09,160
to use your voice?

85
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:11,220
Okay.

86
00:05:11,519 --> 00:05:18,740
So pretty much no one, which is so fascinating, because as Vin said, the voice is the instrument.

87
00:05:19,019 --> 00:05:19,579
It's the instrument.

88
00:05:19,579 --> 00:05:20,100
It is an instrument.

89
00:05:20,220 --> 00:05:23,319
It's one of the most complicated musical instruments there is.

90
00:05:24,860 --> 00:05:27,120
But we're never taught how to play it, right?

91
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:32,399
It's as if I were to hand you an instrument, let's say a violin, and I said, play me a

92
00:05:32,399 --> 00:05:32,639
song.

93
00:05:33,480 --> 00:05:38,879
You wouldn't be able to play me a song, right, on the spot, even if I left you alone with

94
00:05:38,879 --> 00:05:47,560
that instrument for days, weeks, months, years, in our case, right, with our musical instrument.

95
00:05:48,699 --> 00:05:54,139
You're not going to be able to fulfill the potential of that instrument unless you receive

96
00:05:54,600 --> 00:05:57,319
some guidance, some instruction, right?

97
00:05:57,420 --> 00:05:58,879
I think we can all kind of see that.

98
00:05:59,620 --> 00:06:05,379
But then we're born with this incredible instrument, and it's so unique, and it's so interesting,

99
00:06:05,459 --> 00:06:10,060
and it's got all of these components, but we're never really taught how to play it.

100
00:06:11,040 --> 00:06:17,540
And if you want to be able to influence and engage people when you're speaking, you're

101
00:06:17,540 --> 00:06:21,519
with your words, your voice is your most important tool.

102
00:06:23,240 --> 00:06:29,100
Most people, when they're getting ready to give a speech or a presentation, they spend

103
00:06:29,100 --> 00:06:32,379
so much time working out the content of what they're going to say, right?

104
00:06:32,939 --> 00:06:36,720
The outline, okay, what statistics am I going to use?

105
00:06:38,620 --> 00:06:40,199
What's my content, right?

106
00:06:40,279 --> 00:06:41,300
It's all about the content.

107
00:06:41,560 --> 00:06:46,040
But then the delivery kind of gets left to the last minute, or we just say, I'll just

108
00:06:46,040 --> 00:06:46,939
wing the delivery.

109
00:06:47,800 --> 00:06:53,720
But there's substantial evidence that shows that actually the delivery of what you're

110
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:59,699
saying is at least as important, if not more important, than the actual words that you're

111
00:06:59,699 --> 00:06:59,959
saying.

112
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:07,620
So today, we're going to talk about the delivery, and we're going to talk about our voice, and

113
00:07:07,620 --> 00:07:14,259
I want to give you some tools so that you can start to unlock your own voice, and then

114
00:07:14,259 --> 00:07:17,139
hopefully set you on a journey of...

115
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:21,379
Discovering your voice and learning to maximize its potential.

116
00:07:21,540 --> 00:07:26,860
Good breathing is so essential for good vocalism, right?

117
00:07:26,959 --> 00:07:33,759
In the singing world, it's very common for voice teachers to say, 90% of vocal faults

118
00:07:34,259 --> 00:07:37,399
have to do with the breath, or a faulty breath.

119
00:07:38,300 --> 00:07:42,680
And I would say that actually is true in speech as well.

120
00:07:42,959 --> 00:07:45,620
So if we attend to the breath.

121
00:07:45,620 --> 00:07:51,019
If we fix issues with our breathing, a lot of the other technical problems that we encounter

122
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:53,199
are automatically solved.

123
00:07:53,560 --> 00:07:55,139
So we want to make sure that we...

124
00:07:55,139 --> 00:07:55,959
I mean, we could really...

125
00:07:55,959 --> 00:07:59,620
We could spend a whole weekend just talking about breath, but we're going to condense

126
00:07:59,620 --> 00:08:02,240
it and I'm going to give you the most important nuggets.

127
00:08:03,439 --> 00:08:07,079
Good breathing for your voice is going to make you sound more steady.

128
00:08:08,420 --> 00:08:11,120
It's going to make your voice sound rich and resonant.

129
00:08:11,860 --> 00:08:13,959
It's going to make your voice sound more beautiful.

130
00:08:14,740 --> 00:08:20,660
Because a lot of the time, if we have any sounds that are not pretty, it's because

131
00:08:20,660 --> 00:08:21,779
the breath is stuck.

132
00:08:23,199 --> 00:08:29,319
It's also going to be one of the biggest determinants in if you sound confident and if you sound

133
00:08:29,319 --> 00:08:30,680
polished when you speak.

134
00:08:31,379 --> 00:08:32,399
So it's pretty important.

135
00:08:32,720 --> 00:08:38,659
When you walk into a room, the most powerful person in that room is the one with the most

136
00:08:38,659 --> 00:08:39,320
relaxed breath.

137
00:08:40,120 --> 00:08:42,100
Because they're the one that's going to make the best decisions.

138
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:43,440
So...

139
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:47,360
We want to kind of first map out where the diaphragm is.

140
00:08:47,559 --> 00:08:51,980
Because a lot of people, singers included, so many teachers say, sing from your diaphragm,

141
00:08:51,980 --> 00:08:54,179
breathe from your diaphragm, use your diaphragm.

142
00:08:54,179 --> 00:08:55,899
But then they have no idea where the diaphragm is.

143
00:08:56,120 --> 00:08:58,159
It's actually a little higher than we would think.

144
00:08:58,240 --> 00:09:02,139
The diaphragm is kind of like an umbrella shaped muscle underneath the lungs.

145
00:09:02,799 --> 00:09:10,340
So what happens is that when the diaphragm descends on the inhale, it moves our...

146
00:09:10,340 --> 00:09:12,320
We could say our guts out of the way.

147
00:09:12,320 --> 00:09:17,139
And that's why we feel that sense of release and expansion here.

148
00:09:17,379 --> 00:09:19,840
So we can't actually feel the diaphragm.

149
00:09:19,860 --> 00:09:23,440
We're feeling the effect of the diaphragm on our body.

150
00:09:23,700 --> 00:09:26,259
So we could call it a belly breath.

151
00:09:26,360 --> 00:09:28,820
Because we're going to feel a little expansion in the belly.

152
00:09:29,799 --> 00:09:35,080
So just kind of put your hand on your belly, kind of a little bit of your lower belly.

153
00:09:36,159 --> 00:09:40,639
And I want us to see if we can kind of get in touch with this diaphragmatic breath.

154
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:45,700
So what I want us to do is I want us to do what I call a whispered ah.

155
00:09:45,980 --> 00:09:47,019
It sounds like this.

156
00:09:50,889 --> 00:09:52,750
As you do that, what happens to your stomach?

157
00:09:54,700 --> 00:09:55,539
It goes in.

158
00:09:55,620 --> 00:09:55,879
Right.

159
00:09:56,139 --> 00:09:56,500
Exactly.

160
00:09:56,720 --> 00:09:57,220
It's an innie.

161
00:09:58,120 --> 00:09:59,519
So we're going to do that whispered ah.

162
00:09:59,580 --> 00:10:05,059
I always like to start with the exhale, because if you exhale well, the inhale takes care

163
00:10:05,059 --> 00:10:05,419
of itself.

164
00:10:06,200 --> 00:10:06,559
Right?

165
00:10:06,600 --> 00:10:07,679
So we start with the exhale.

166
00:10:08,500 --> 00:10:12,220
And what I want you to do when you get to the bottom of your exhale, you've exhaled,

167
00:10:12,779 --> 00:10:14,059
let's say 85.

168
00:10:15,059 --> 00:10:17,519
We don't want you to exhale all of your air.

169
00:10:18,600 --> 00:10:20,559
Actually it's impossible to exhale all of your air.

170
00:10:20,639 --> 00:10:22,919
But we're going to exhale mostly.

171
00:10:23,360 --> 00:10:27,600
And then you're going to wait just a second at the bottom of the exhale.

172
00:10:29,399 --> 00:10:31,679
Wait for that urge to breathe.

173
00:10:32,019 --> 00:10:32,500
Okay?

174
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:39,759
And then all you do is you let that belly release like you're filling up a small balloon

175
00:10:39,759 --> 00:10:40,379
in your stomach.

176
00:10:40,860 --> 00:10:43,320
And if you want to start by breathing through the nose.

177
00:10:43,320 --> 00:10:47,960
Some people find they're able to get a more relaxed, automatic breath through the nose.

178
00:10:48,059 --> 00:10:49,019
That's completely fine.

179
00:10:50,480 --> 00:10:53,139
Eventually you're going to want to train yourself to also breathe through your mouth.

180
00:10:53,679 --> 00:10:57,379
Because when you're speaking, you don't really have time to breathe through your nose.

181
00:10:58,679 --> 00:11:00,080
It's mostly going to be through your mouth.

182
00:11:00,240 --> 00:11:01,460
But feel free to start with the nose.

183
00:11:01,860 --> 00:11:03,200
So does everyone feel that?

184
00:11:04,240 --> 00:11:08,759
So the little cheat sheet is that the exhale is the innie.

185
00:11:10,519 --> 00:11:12,279
The inhale is the outie.

186
00:11:12,500 --> 00:11:12,899
Right?

187
00:11:14,360 --> 00:11:21,120
So one little pitfall of this is you have to be okay with letting your gut fall out.

188
00:11:21,299 --> 00:11:21,559
Right?

189
00:11:21,679 --> 00:11:22,740
So we're in L.A.

190
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:24,580
Everyone's like holding it in.

191
00:11:24,799 --> 00:11:25,179
Right?

192
00:11:25,340 --> 00:11:30,120
Or, you know, as women, a lot of times I think we feel this pressure to keep this sucked in.

193
00:11:30,539 --> 00:11:33,419
But then you're never going to get a diaphragmatic breath.

194
00:11:33,799 --> 00:11:40,240
Because you're not allowing, your abdominal muscles are tightening and not allowing the diaphragm to do its thing.

195
00:11:40,820 --> 00:11:42,240
So we kind of have to.

196
00:11:43,120 --> 00:11:45,159
Let those ab muscles release.

197
00:11:45,299 --> 00:11:46,360
And if you have a six pack.

198
00:11:46,519 --> 00:11:47,519
Anyone here have a six pack?

199
00:11:48,360 --> 00:11:51,179
That, you know, it can be sometimes a little tricky to let the abs release.

200
00:11:51,480 --> 00:11:51,700
But.

201
00:11:52,779 --> 00:11:56,899
So we're going to do a couple little exercises to practice this diaphragmatic breath.

202
00:11:58,179 --> 00:11:59,720
So we're going to first start with a sh.

203
00:12:02,269 --> 00:12:03,090
Can you all try that?

204
00:12:04,309 --> 00:12:04,669
Sh.

205
00:12:07,320 --> 00:12:08,440
And do a couple in a row.

206
00:12:10,340 --> 00:12:12,080
Practicing the in and out of the belly.

207
00:12:12,879 --> 00:12:13,240
Good.

208
00:12:13,340 --> 00:12:15,059
Can you make the sh a little bit.

209
00:12:15,059 --> 00:12:17,740
Now more intense?

210
00:12:18,059 --> 00:12:18,480
Sh.

211
00:12:22,230 --> 00:12:22,669
Right.

212
00:12:23,529 --> 00:12:27,789
So one of the ways that we increase volume is through the use of our air.

213
00:12:27,929 --> 00:12:30,549
And I want you to think of this as your accelerator.

214
00:12:30,990 --> 00:12:32,149
It's like your gas pedal.

215
00:12:32,269 --> 00:12:32,450
Right?

216
00:12:32,789 --> 00:12:34,269
So you can go sh.

217
00:12:36,919 --> 00:12:37,360
Right?

218
00:12:37,419 --> 00:12:41,419
Depending on how you want your voice to sound.

219
00:12:41,759 --> 00:12:43,480
So let's try the hiss now.

220
00:12:43,620 --> 00:12:44,120
Same idea.

221
00:12:44,259 --> 00:12:44,500
Ready?

222
00:12:45,039 --> 00:12:45,480
Sss.

223
00:12:48,879 --> 00:12:50,059
And do a couple in a row.

224
00:13:02,940 --> 00:13:05,000
And you're really working on your inhale.

225
00:13:05,639 --> 00:13:06,759
Inhale being automatic.

226
00:13:07,039 --> 00:13:07,240
Right?

227
00:13:07,320 --> 00:13:08,179
It's like a reflex.

228
00:13:08,639 --> 00:13:10,279
It's not something that you have to think about.

229
00:13:10,419 --> 00:13:15,820
And I never want any of my singers or speakers to think about getting a big breath.

230
00:13:16,220 --> 00:13:17,539
That's one of our pitfalls.

231
00:13:17,899 --> 00:13:20,840
Almost every singer has that a little bit mixed up.

232
00:13:21,159 --> 00:13:22,399
It's not about a big breath.

233
00:13:22,919 --> 00:13:24,940
Because a big breath actually creates more tension.

234
00:13:25,620 --> 00:13:27,240
But it's about a deep breath.

235
00:13:28,100 --> 00:13:32,019
And if the breath is deep and small, it's going to be good.

236
00:13:32,179 --> 00:13:34,980
And it actually will be bigger than what you were saying.

237
00:13:34,980 --> 00:13:36,100
A clavicular breath.

238
00:13:37,899 --> 00:13:38,820
From the shoulders.

239
00:13:39,320 --> 00:13:39,620
So.

240
00:13:40,840 --> 00:13:41,519
Excuse me.

241
00:13:41,639 --> 00:13:42,000
Okay.

242
00:13:42,039 --> 00:13:44,539
So now, let's put our voice with it.

243
00:13:44,679 --> 00:13:47,820
Because the s and the sh were unvoiced consonants.

